+PATCHED VERSION — deliberate, documented deviation from Jiang et al.'s released script.
+The token-classification methodology (find_modified_text_spans / semantic_reward_char_spans /
+classify_tokens) is copied verbatim, unchanged. What changed, and why:
+
+1. Running-sum accumulation instead of per-token lists. The original keeps every single
+   classified token's per-layer activation vector as an individual tensor in a growing Python
+   list (per_fn[label][reward_fn][layer].append(...)) for the ENTIRE run, only reducing to a
+   mean at the very end via torch.stack(...).mean(). Since mean = sum/count, this is
+   mathematically unnecessary — a running (sum, count) per reward_fn/layer/side is sufficient
+   and bounded in size (50 functions x 65 layers x 5120 dims x 2 sides, ~133MB total,
+   regardless of how many conversations/tokens are processed). This is very likely what was
+   silently exhausting memory on a 380-conversation run.
+2. Periodic checkpointing (every CHECKPOINT_EVERY conversations) with resume support — the
+   original writes exactly one output file at the very end and has zero resumability; a
+   kill/crash at conversation 250/380 loses everything. This patch saves the running sums +
+   counts + the set of already-processed conversation IDs to disk periodically (atomic
+   write-then-rename), and on startup loads any existing checkpoint and skips conversations
+   already accounted for.
+3. Live memory printing (current process RSS via /proc/self/status) at every checkpoint, so
+   any future silent death can be correlated with a specific memory level and conversation
+   index instead of being a mystery.
+
+Runs Qwen3-8B (or --model override) over every conversation and, in each conversation's first
+post-discovery paragraph (the first paragraph written AFTER the model figured out the hidden
+criterion), splits the tokens around the reward words:
 
     [... before tokens ...] [reward tokens] [... after tokens ...]
 
